## Verdict: `new` — contract `regulate` (kind=exchange, family=fields, set=cell, maps=[])

The entry arrived with a live **skeptic dispute** claiming this is a `refinement` of `signal`. I engaged it head-on rather than rubber-stamping either side. My conclusion: **the skeptic is right about the defect, wrong about the fix.**

**What was wrong:** the prior `why:` named `decay`/`pacemaker` as the closest contracts and never mentioned `signal` — which is genuinely the nearest neighbour (the *only* other registered per-agent internal-state ODE of the firing-rate/recurrent-network class). That's a real hole, and I rewrote `why:` to lead with `signal`.

**Why `new` still stands** — the dispute's own `what_would_settle_it` sets the bar: a widening survives only if it does *not* break `signal`'s connectome/engine-integration identity. It can't:

| | `signal` (registered) | `regulate` (this) |
|---|---|---|
| coupling | `synapse` EDGE-SET via `pre`/`post` maps — "the maps are PART of the signature" | dense per-operator matrix, `maps=[]`, **zero** cell-to-cell coupling (intracellular) |
| integration | `EMIT=velocity`; the ENGINE integrates | SELF-SOLVES the macro-step (adaptive Dopri5), returns exact `y(dt)-y0` delta |
| forcing | recurrent presynaptic input only | reads a FIXED sensed driver `u` (`W_in@u`) |
| kind | lateral | exchange |

Expressing `regulate` through `signal` forces changing its set, emptying its maps, dropping the edge-set, adding a forcing read, and flipping engine-integration to self-solve — five signature changes that dissolve `signal`'s identity and break every existing connectome user. So the mismatch **forces a distinct operator → `new` stands.** They're two contracts of the same recurrent-network *family*, not two implementations of one contract.

