## What I did

**Verdict: `new`, `implementation_of: adhere`** (not the `alias → attraction_repulsion` the parent/Morse/Hertzian/Harmonic majority chose, nor the `refinement` my predecessor's draft note landed on).

The decision hinged on a factual question I resolved **at the source**, reading `src/plexus/operators/attraction_repulsion.py` directly. The registered operator is a D'Orsogna self-propelled-**particle** velocity law:
- `set="particle"`, `EMIT="velocity"` — a hand-coded velocity law, not an energy
- required **global scalar** `sigma` (a free knob), **per-TYPE** `p` coupling
- reads `pos/occ/edge_index/node_type`, **never `radius`**; no energy, no virial, no size-consistent contact distance

So `alias` is factually false (it flatters the language), and hosting LJ isn't a bounded widening — it changes `set`, `emit`, the coupling ontology (per-type→per-cell), the range ontology (global knob→physical `sigma=r_i+r_j` from radius), and adds an energy readout. That deletes the D'Orsogna model → the record's `new` test, not `refinement`. The six pair potentials share one signature and differ only in `U(r)`, so they collapse to **one** new contract `adhere` with several implementations — non-inflating. LJ, being adhesive, fits `adhere` more directly than the repulsion-only SoftSphere that already landed `new → adhere` (dispute-survived, order 15), so I aligned with that source-grounded sibling rather than the source-false alias majority.

**Contract:** `adhere` (lateral / interaction / cell), reads position + radius + alive + epsilon coupling, writes position; maps = the half-summed pairwise autodiff-force plus the size-consistent additive contact distance with the r_min 12-6 well and its C1 cutoff.

The note carries the one-paragraph verdict plus the single strongest argument against it — the `refinement` position (widen the registered attraction-repulsion slot rather than mint a parallel contract), and why I reject it against the frozen, promoted baseline that rule 4 pins the comparison to.

